  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A plume of tropical moisture characterized by \n2\"+ PWATs and dewpoints in the 70s will continue to overspread \nthe Carolinas to the east of an advancing cold front through \nthis evening. Special 11/18Z KMHX sounding analyzed a record\nhigh PW of 2.37\" this afternoon. Tornado watch in effect until\n8pm this evening as this rich tropical airmass in tandem with\nstrong low level helicity with backed sfc flow will continue the\ntor threat. Overlap of best dynamics and supercell coverage will\ncontinue to be over the inland Coastal Plain counties, though a\ntornado cannot be ruled out further east as well through early\nthis evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The remnants of Isaias are forecast to round \nthe upper level ridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic \nCoast early next week. Drier air then moves in behind it with \ndrying conditions developing through the upcoming week. With the\ndrier air in place, overnight lows should be seasonbly cool, but\nbuilding hts will lead to above climo high temps in the upper\n70s to low 80s early in the week, and then even higher temps in\nthe upper 80s to near 90 inland by mid week. Mostly sunny skies\nwith high pressure will keep any rain chances away.",
